### Why is case-insensitive sorting needed?

Natural String ordering compares by character codes. All uppercase letters (A-Z, codes 65-90) come before all lowercase letters (a-z, codes 97-122), so "Zebra" sorts before "apple". This is clearly wrong for a human-readable alphabetical list.

### How do you sort names case-insensitively?

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class CaseInsensitiveSort {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> names = List.of("Rajesh", "amit", "PRIYA", "kavita", "Suresh");

        List<String> sorted = names.stream()
            .sorted(String.CASE_INSENSITIVE_ORDER)
            .collect(Collectors.toList());

        System.out.println(sorted);
    }
}
```

```text
[amit, kavita, PRIYA, Rajesh, Suresh]
```

### How do you sort Employee objects by name case-insensitively?

```java
List<Employee> sorted = staff.stream()
    .sorted(Comparator.comparing(Employee::getName,
        String.CASE_INSENSITIVE_ORDER))
    .collect(Collectors.toList());
```

### How does case-insensitive compare to case-sensitive?

| Input | Case-sensitive sort | Case-insensitive sort |
|---|---|---|
| ["Zebra", "apple", "Banana"] | [Banana, Zebra, apple] | [apple, Banana, Zebra] |
| ["PRIYA", "amit", "Rajesh"] | [PRIYA, Rajesh, amit] | [amit, PRIYA, Rajesh] |

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake: using natural order when names have inconsistent casing.

```java
// WRONG: natural order groups uppercase before lowercase
names.stream().sorted().collect(Collectors.toList());
// [PRIYA, Rajesh, Suresh, amit, kavita]  ← wrong!

// CORRECT: case-insensitive order
names.stream()
    .sorted(String.CASE_INSENSITIVE_ORDER)
    .collect(Collectors.toList());
// [amit, kavita, PRIYA, Rajesh, Suresh]  ← correct!
```

### What about normalising with toLowerCase?

An equivalent approach extracts a normalised key.

```java
// Alternative: compare by lower-cased key
List<Employee> sorted = staff.stream()
    .sorted(Comparator.comparing(e -> e.getName().toLowerCase()))
    .collect(Collectors.toList());
```

This allocates a new lowercase string per comparison, whereas `CASE_INSENSITIVE_ORDER` does not. For large datasets the constant is more efficient.

### When do you need a Collator instead?

Case-insensitive is still not fully locale-correct for all languages. For truly locale-aware ordering, use a `Collator`.

```java
import java.text.Collator;
import java.util.Locale;

Collator collator = Collator.getInstance(new Locale("hi", "IN"));

List<String> hindiSorted = names.stream()
    .sorted(collator)
    .collect(Collectors.toList());
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- `String.CASE_INSENSITIVE_ORDER` is a pre-built `Comparator<String>`, not a method call.
- It works for English but may not produce correct results for Turkish, German, or other languages with special case-folding rules.
- The two-argument `Comparator.comparing(keyExtractor, keyComparator)` is the idiomatic way to sort objects by a key using a custom comparator.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "What is the difference between natural order and case-insensitive?" Natural order uses character codes; case-insensitive folds case.
- "When is a Collator needed?" For locale-specific alphabetical rules.
- "Does case-insensitive sort change the original strings?" No, it only changes the comparison logic.
